{"id":"precision-recall-curve-and-average-precision","t":"Precision-recall curve and average precision","tags":["Metrics & Evaluation","Classification"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"pr_curve","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A precision-recall curve shows the trade-off a classifier offers as its decision threshold moves. Average precision (AP) summarises it as a step-wise area.\n\nWrite `pr_curve(y_true, scores)` that returns `(precision, recall, thresholds, ap)`.\n\n## Definitions\n- `thresholds`: the **distinct** values of `scores`, in **descending** order.\n- At threshold $t$, predict positive for every row with score $\\ge t$. Then $\\text{precision}_t = TP/(TP + FP)$ and $\\text{recall}_t = TP / n_+$, where $n_+$ is the number of positive labels.\n- `precision[k]` and `recall[k]` belong to `thresholds[k]`.\n- $$\\mathrm{AP} = \\sum_k (R_k - R_{k-1})\\,P_k, \\qquad R_{-1} = 0.$$\n\n## Input / output\n- `y_true`: int array `(n,)` of 0/1 with at least one 1; `scores`: float array `(n,)`.\n- Return three float arrays of the same length (one entry per distinct score) and `ap` as a Python `float`.\n- Vectorise with a sort and cumulative sums: no loop over thresholds.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef pr_curve(y_true, scores):\n    \"\"\"Return (precision, recall, thresholds descending, average precision).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Sort by score descending. Then \"predict positive for the top $j$ rows\" is just a prefix, and TP and FP for every prefix come from two cumulative sums.","Tied scores must enter together, because a threshold can't split them. Keep only the **last** index of each run of equal sorted scores.","AP: `np.sum(np.diff(recall, prepend=0) * precision)`. Recall only increases at thresholds that add a positive, so steps that add only negatives contribute 0."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef pr_curve(y_true, scores):\n    y, s = np.asarray(y_true, dtype=float), np.asarray(scores, dtype=float)\n    order = np.argsort(-s, kind='stable')\n    s, y = s[order], y[order]\n    tp, fp = np.cumsum(y), np.cumsum(1 - y)\n    # the last row of each run of equal scores closes that threshold: everything >= t is included\n    last = np.r_[np.flatnonzero(s[1:] != s[:-1]), len(s) - 1]\n    tp, fp = tp[last], fp[last]\n    precision, recall = tp / (tp + fp), tp / y.sum()\n    ap = np.sum(np.diff(recall, prepend=0.0) * precision)\n    return precision, recall, s[last], float(ap)\n","ex_md":"## Why prefixes\nThresholding at $t$ selects exactly the rows whose score is at least $t$, which after a descending sort is a prefix. So one sort and two `cumsum`s give TP and FP for every threshold in $O(n\\log n)$. Ties are the subtle part: a run of equal scores is all-in or all-out, so only the end of each run is a valid operating point (test 2 and test 5).\n\n## Why this AP formula\nAP is the area under the step function that holds precision $P_k$ over the recall interval $(R_{k-1}, R_k]$. It's the precision averaged over the positives, each positive weighted by when it's recovered: with no ties, AP equals the mean of precision@rank over the ranks of the positives. It doesn't interpolate linearly between points, which would be over-optimistic in PR space.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(n\\log n)$ for the sort; everything else is linear.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Emitting one point per row instead of per distinct score.\n- Using `>` instead of `>=`, which shifts every threshold by one row.\n- Trapezoidal integration (`np.trapz`) of the PR curve: that's a different, optimistic quantity.\n- Starting the recall at the first point instead of 0, which drops the first step of the area.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why is PR-AUC more informative than ROC-AUC under heavy class imbalance? (False positives are measured against predicted positives, not against the huge negative pool.)\n- What's the expected AP of a random classifier? (The positive rate $n_+/n$, unlike ROC-AUC's fixed 0.5.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([1, 0, 1, 0])\narray([0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.1])","out":"(array([1.        , 0.5       , 0.66666667, 0.5       ]), array([0.5, 0.5, 1. , 1. ]), array([0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.1]), 0.8333333333333333)"},{"in":"array([0, 1, 1])\narray([0.5, 0.5, 0.2])","out":"(array([0.5       , 0.66666667]), array([0.5, 1. ]), array([0.5, 0.2]), 0.5833333333333333)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../system-design-101/index.html#l09-09","t":"Search evaluation metrics","c":"System Design"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l06-02","t":"ROC and precision-recall curves","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l06-06","t":"Imbalanced data","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["auc-roc","precision-recall-at-k"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1, 0, 1, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.1], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [1.0, 0.5, 0.6666666666666666, 0.5], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.5, 1.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.1], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.8333333333333333]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [0, 1, 1], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.5, 0.2], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.6666666666666666], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 0.2], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.5833333333333333]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1, 1, 0, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [4.0, 3.0, 2.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [1.0, 1.0, 0.6666666666666666, 0.5], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.5, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [4.0, 3.0, 2.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [0, 0, 1, 1], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [4.0, 3.0, 2.0, 1.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.0, 0.0, 0.3333333333333333, 0.5], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0], \"dtype\": 